THE CASE AGAINST THE TARGET:
SRC_001 computes the intersection of per-school grounded extensions and commits only that intersection, claiming reinstatement survives. Consider two projections where node C is attacked only by A and A is attacked only by B. In projection 1, an extra attacker E attacks B, so B is refuted, A is reinstated, and C is reinstated. In projection 2, B has no extra attacker, so B is accepted, A is refuted, and C is suspended. The intersection suspends C. Yet every individual projection either reinstates C (projection 1) or refutes its sole attacker A (projection 2), and in the latter C's suspension is a non-derived artifact. The proposal's refutation-union rule commits C as refuted only when it is outside the union, but C is outside neither projection's grounded extension, so it remains suspended in the intersection while being reinstated in projection 1. Reinstatement is therefore not merely re-derived; it fails for a node that is reinstated in at least one projection, directly violating the reinstatement guarantee the problem requires be preserved.

TARGET:
Strengthen skepticism across conditioning regimes by computing the grounded extension on a family of school-projected attack subgraphs — each projection a deterministic function of the graph per registered school (1958b39701612057) — and committing only the intersection of all grounded extensions; refutation then commits only for nodes outside the union. Determinism and polynomial cost hold if the school set is fixed and each projection is linear in edge count. The criticizable point: the intersection of several grounded extensions is not itself a Dung grounded extension, so reinstatement must be re-derived and may fail on nodes that are reinstated in every projection yet suspended in the intersection, weakening the very guarantee being extended.